



/>>



The concept of "/Research Objects/" emerged during the first two decades of 
this century in relation to developments such as the 
observed "/replication crisis/" that began to catch scientists' 
attention at this time %-- specifically, the 
difficulties faced by fellow investigators in reproducing the 
results of (even well-executed) published research work.  A concomitant 
and related notion is "/data transparency/", again related to 
priorities for researchers to document as clearly and openly 
as possible the protocols, data acquisition methods, and 
analytic processes (and assumptions) that underlie published 
findings.  So-called "/Minimum Information Standards/" 
such as MIBBI (Minimum Information for Biological and 
Biomedical Investigations) provide a kind of checklist 
for authors to ensure desired levels and comprehensiveness 
of data transparency.  Simultaneously, open-access 
data sets and deposition of raw data files in 
special-purposes curation sites (Open Science Framework, 
Dataverse, Dryad, etc.) has become commonplace, at 
least in principle.  Publishers often encourage 
(or even require) authors to ensure that raw data is 
available to any one reading research articles, or at 
least to explain explicitly why open data sharing 
is impossible in specific cases.  Some funding 
sources (e.g., the Bill and Melinda Gates Foundation) 
similarly require commit to data transparency as a 
precondition for research teams to have their work supported.  

The term Research Object in itself was apparently coined 
in 2013 to describe a proposed specification for 
how the totality of information related to a scientific 
publication should be packaged.  The underlying idea, 
of course, predates that.  It has become evident for a while 
now that the basic unit of research dissemination is not a 
single document (published article, book, or chapter) 
but rather a collection of text documents, data 
files, potentially analytic code, and (often) 
multimedia materials and graphics; such resources 
collectively should serve as a single entity that 
may be cited, referenced, and downloaded, and users 
should find it straightforward to browse the 
disparate content types and find that information 
or presentations most relevant to them.  The 
Research Object specification is one proposal 
to facilitate these goals, but the general term 
"/research object/" may be used for any package 
that is deliberately structured around data-transparency 
and multi-modal publishing in general (insofar as 
text documents are, in the typical case, understood 
to be accompanied by other content types).

Meanwhile, "/translational science/" has a similar timeline.  
It seems that "/translational medicine/" as a phrase 
first appeared in the 1990's (although the concept is 
older), referencing protocols that 
emphasize practical applications of biomedical research 
and "/bench to bedside/" translation %-- once 
specific clinical interventions or other scientific 
breakthroughs are consolidated in a theoretical 
and/or experimental context, there are then  
further steps needed to deploy novel paradigms 
for concrete benefits.  Almost all scientific research, 
to be sure, is motivated by concern for the social good 
at some level, but the idea behind translational science 
is that self-identified academic focus and specialization 
can augment the probability that practical benefits 
are realized judiciously, in contrast to rather inchoate 
notions of the scientific knowledge's long-term advantages.  
For example, translational scientists have identified 
five general stages in the evolution of health-care 
interventions from abstract ideas to established 
practice %-- the so-called "/translational continuum"/ %-- of which 
the fourth involves commonplace 
adoption of (once-)novel practices in clinical settings 
and the fifth marks concerted efforts to adopt useful interventions in a wide variety of settings (e.g., not restricted to experimental protocols 
available only in a few hospitals, or to a small, select 
population).  Such models suggest roadmaps 
to help guide deployment once research progresses through 
stages of concrete implementation.  Project might unfold 
in roughly this pattern organically, but establishing 
the continuum as a technical model can help the 
progress to advance expeditiously.

Along similar lines, translational science is concerned 
with informatics themes as far as managing data to 
gauge the effectiveness of practical deployments; 
establishing concrete goals and benchmarks as an intrinsic 
dimension of research applicability, plus methods to check 
how empirical results compare to initial projections; 
structuring research to scale up and/or replicate 
concrete implementations; and other ways of 
including practical considerations as intrinsic to the 
foundational research work.  For example, computer 
code and software used (or developed) to analyze 
originary data sets might be repurposed into 
point-of-care modules in concrete deployment contexts.  
As guided by translational concerns, scientists should 
be attentive when setting up and then operating within 
their overall investigatory ecosystem %-- the equipment, 
technology, software, technology, data sources, etc. %-- 
to how such lab/academic settings can be translated to 
operational environments once abstract research progresses 
to concrete, socially-mediated practice.

Although "/transational medicine/" might be the 
most recognizable term, the usefulness of this 
phrasing has extended to other disciplines.  Thus 
we have, for instance, translational chemistry, 
translational engineering, translational sustainability, 
translational humanities, and so forth.  Such 
fields (more or less neatly delineated as subdisciplines) 
focus in on dimensions of their parent specializations 
(chemistry, etc.) that are particularly relevant to 
social improvement, practical benefits, applied 
research, and so forth.  Translational chemistry, 
as a case in point, emphasizes population health 
and ecological issues related to pollution and 
contamination %-- and insofar as principles 
of chemistry clarify the causative mechanisms 
behind compounds' hazards to people and ecosystems 
%-- while also focusing on concrete 
practices to correct social and environmental damage 
(e.g., site remediation) and develop new, better 
forms of molecules and materials (sustainable 
manufacturing, architecture, pharmaceuticals, etc.).  

Translational science is inherently interdisciplinary, 
and often challenges the divisions commonly 
recognized between humanities and natural sciences.  
Theoretical models (such as the translational continuum) aside, 
many of the concrete issues affecting concrete deployments 
are cultural and socioeconomic in nature.  For example, the 
resources and standards of care available in "/inner-city/" 
hospitals differ noticeably from wealthier community's medical 
centers.  Practical challenges when planning 
socially-beneficial implementations of health-care 
ideas driven by new research, as such, derive 
to an important degree from such imbalances in wealth and 
political influence, and the funding, community, and 
institutional infrastructure that can overcome 
such disparities in the translation 
of fortuitous research to operational practice.

It should be obvious that the rationales behind 
Research Objects and Translational Science overlap in 
significant ways.  These specific terms consolidate 
trends in science and academia that are broadly applicable, 
regardless of how scholars involves specifically 
conceputalize them.  A lot of research work is  
motivated by concerns parallel to those of translational 
medicine, whether or not this wording is specifically 
chosen.  Likewise, any author who plans to develop 
a structure set of supplemental materials in consort 
with a single publication (a traditional human-readable 
text document) is confronting challenges that motivate 
the Research Object model.  Moreover, researchers 
committed (explicitly or not) to translational 
principles are likely to structure presentations 
of the work in the multi-modal fashion associated 
with open-access information sharing and data transparency.  
In short, there are strong grounds for formulating 
"/Translational Research Object/" protocols that 
address both of these areas, with application beyond 
just those projects that explicitly invoke 
each one individually. 

Given the socioeconomic issues recently mentioned, 
one might also stipulate that both Translational 
Science and Research Objects reflect an ongoing 
evolution in the assessment of and institutional 
logistics for investigations.  If present trends 
continue, the individual peer-reviewed article 
is likely to lose its status as the pre-eminent 
certificate of merit for individuals as well 
as research projects in general.  This reflects 
both technical and social factors.  On the 
one hand, to the degree that the documentary 
and pedagogical manifestation of research work 
expands outside individual publications to 
encompass data packages and multimedia content, 
the procedures for peer review and assessment 
need to change accordingly.  Evidently, 
common practice (still) is for reviewers to 
evaluate */papers/*, but largely pass over 
data sets, computer code, or GUI materials %-- which 
could in principle represent a more substantial 
authors' time commitment and innovation 
compared to, say, a single journal article itself.  
Meanwhile, the ideal settings for translational 
science are not restricted to academic labs or 
campuses as commonly understood, and the provenance, 
funding, and credentials associated with translational 
research may not fit conventional assessment models.  
For instance, the majority of work and observations 
critical to a paper on public health may have been 
performed by scientists or technicians 
(or, say, social workers) at an inner-city 
hospital, rather than either faculty or students 
at a university.  

Part of translational science's 
entrenchment may, indeed, be the migration 
of academic practices and expertise outside the 
college campus into a broader spectrum of institutions 
and social environments.  This is not to downplay the 
special focus necessary to conduct science on a rigorous, 
academic level.  Specialists with hands-on expertise 
in practical settings may not have the theoretical 
instincts to triangulate the observations or 
day-to-day functioning of practical deployment 
against the abstract structures of a particular 
field if scholarship.  Extra work is needed to 
marshal practical know-how, experience, and information 
into data relevant to theory-confirmation (or disconfirmation).  
At the same time, practitioners of abstract research 
do not necessarily have (or develop) expertise 
forged from everyday practice in institutional/implementational 
settings.  Translational science points to a hybrid 
academic culture that synthesizes elements from both 
on-campus and "/in-the-field/" environments.

As a way of grounding this (itself somewhat abstract) 
discussion, consider the phenomnon of open-source code 
libraries.  GIT repositories, for example, are 
not peer-reviewed, and indeed content available on a platform 
such as Github can vary substantially in quality and interest 
to relevant communities.  Some repos may be essentially 
just some person's schoolwork or experimentation for 
personal use.  On the other hand, some reposities 
created by a single individual are substantial 
resources that deserve attention comparable to an influential 
book or article, and other excellent libraries merge 
the contributions of many experts.  The variegated 
status and purposes of repos does not, in general, prevent 
programmers and scientists from finding open-access code 
relevant to their work.  A quick look at "/readme/" files 
can usually clarify whether a particular repository 
is intended as a serious contribution envision for 
use by multiple third parties, or a work-in-progress toward 
that end, or rather something much smaller.  And in the 
former category a review of the actual code can serve as an 
indication of the project's overall status %-- 
whether it is actively maintained, compatible with current 
compiler and language standards, interopable with other 
code bases, and so forth.  Successful projects often acquire 
their own momentum insofar as additional programmers can add 
their own components or adaptations %-- porting to 
new environments, scripting-language wrappers, adding 
new functionality, etc. %-- thereby expanding the 
scope and usability of the project overall.  Open-source 
libraries (and the coders who create them) thereby acquire a 
hierarchy of status and influence in an emergent manner.  
Notions of merit are not dependent on subjective impressions 
of peer reviewers nor on institutional bureaucracies.

Obviously, meanwhile, open-source code implicitly adhered to 
the (more recent) "/diamond/" open-access model.  Neither 
programmers nor users face financial burdens at least 
for the basic hosting and cloning of repositories 
(free as in "/beer/" as well as "/speech/").

Of course, technical facets of the computing ecosystem play a 
role too.  Object files either compile or don't; 
test suites pass or fail.  A reviewer reading a 
late-stage manuscript draft relies on personal impressions 
as to whether the writing fits in familiar ways into 
the disciplinary contexts which the paper evokes, 
as far as whether terminology, methods, analyses, 
and presuppositions are characteristic of the 
expected audience (or if deviations from those paradigms 
are noted and explained).  Reviewers need to judge whether 
article introduce new ideas and whether these open 
avenues for additional research.  This process is subjective, 
especially insofar as the reviewer is working solely with a 
text document.  An open-source library, conversely, 
can be manipulated more tangibly: one can follow 
documented instructed, compile the sources, run test 
suites, etc., and eventually launch whatever executable 
binaries are produced.  Such hands-on activity offers a lot 
of feedback beyond subjective impressions of code quality.

This comparison between peer-reviewed articles and open-source 
code libraries is relevant to contemporary publishing 
because, one might opine, disseminating research in 
the context of translational science is more like 
maintaining and open-source library than preparing  
a submission for a refereed journal.  Those who 
develop translational-science publications, presumably, 
are often immersed in concrete practice (outside 
the scope of academic institution per se) and seek 
to impose investigatory discipline on the information 
and observations that are implicitly available 
in their logistical environment.  Mining the 
"/ambient/" data that exists in clinical, governmental, or 
nonprofit settings is a different pathway to rigorous 
science that constructing artificial data-acquisition 
locales (e.g., research labs).  Such differences also 
affect the initiation and evolution of research projects.  
A graduate student progressing toward a doctoral 
dissertation, for example, will go through a multi-step 
process of exploring topics, finding an adviser, 
forming a comittee, project approval, and so forth, 
each of which has the result of outsiders molding 
and nudging the eventual work in particular paradigmatic 
directions.  Contrast this to a hypothetical 
social worker or medical humanities specialist who 
works in a clinical or governmental setting and, 
with insights shaped by their day-to-day experience, 
decide to seek out public-access data sets and similar 
empirical resources to help translate their 
emerging (on-the-ground-based) personal perspectives into 
systematic presentations accorded scientific merit.  

Publisher need to be aware of the special status evinced 
by translational and multi-disciplinary research so 
that they can best adopt dissemination practices 
proper to the goals of translational sciences, 
and best support authors and investigators who 
pursue them.  For example, researchers traditionally 
have a personal stake in publications because they 
represent a larger community's endorsement of 
someone's general knowledge and competence as a scientist.  
Having a paper accepted by a high-impact journal 
not only means that other will read one's words, but also 
confers a measure of prestige and recognition.  
In a more translational context %-- where a single 
document is not only the basic unit of dissemination 
%-- considerations are somewhat different.  Multi-faceted 
publications documenting the practical success of an 
innovative project, for instance, are valuable to 
the authors not primarily for the venue's name-recognition 
(with respect to a particular scientist looking for 
future grants, tenure, etc.) but because researcher 
are motivated to continue and expand operations 
on the ground (where funding might come from 
government programs for social good, or nonprofits).  
The typical audience for publications in the latter 
context is not necessarily an acedemic reader 
familiar with a particular document format 
(abstract, similar-research statement, summary of 
findings, etc.) but rather interested 
parties from multiple backgrounds (nonprofit 
administrators, civil servants, software engineers) 
who may be most interested in the nontraditional 
resources of a publication package: data sets, 
computer code, statistical graphics, and so forth.  

All of these points have tangible consequences 
for publishing technology as well.  Translational 
science implies not only an intellectual 
transition to multi-disciplinary research 
but also a physical displacement of the 
scientific environment away from academic 
and laboratory settings and toward on-the-ground 
implementations (plus communities of independent 
researchers).  In terms of digital artifacts, 
this is mirrored in the weight of dissimenation 
shiting from conventonal text documents to 
computational and multimedia artifacts.  At 
one level, this means that a reader seeking to 
learn about a public health, or environmental 
remediation, or government/nonprofit partnership 
initiative (as examples) might look 
toward videos, code libaries, "/story maps"/, or 
other digital resources in addition to 
reading a journal-style article.  At another level, 
the expertise and innovations manifest in research 
projects might be expressed in such "/supplemental/" 
materials more than papers themselves.  Publishing 
software therefore bears the burden of helping 
authors develop such materials to the highest quality 
possible.  The continued funding and deployment 
of projects may depend on the success of content 
such as code libraries, data-acquisition, information 
management, and empirical review all of which 
are unified (in whole or in part) into publication 
packages.

So, to bring this introductory discussion to a close, 
contemporary publishing technology needs to 
support authors in a variety of contexts and to 
help authors produce a variety of content 
%-- text documents but also multimedia, analytic 
and GUI codedata sets, and so on.  Such requirements 
apply in particular to code that may be published 
as part of a Research Object (or similar 
bundle format).  There are at least five functions 
that such dataset-oriented code might perform:   

%/ desc

Deserialization:  Perhaps the most obvious use for 
dataset code is reading raw data files themselves.  
Individual scientific disciplines are often associated with domain-specific data publishing formats that are paired with dedicated deserialization tools (i.e., computer code which reads raw data files into live memory, without
relying on special-purpose software). Such code libraries are often maintained by individual institutions or organizations — which
might be academic departments, research labs, or independent groups — on behalf of a broader research community. Examples include (among many others) FASTQ and VCF for genomics; HDF5 and ROOT for physics; FITS and NETCDF in astronomy and earth sciences;
PDB and MOL in biochemistry; IFC and BIM for architecture/engineering; SEGY and BAG for geophysics and oceanography; CONLL-U in
computational linguistics; DICOM and OMETIFF for bioimaging; and GIS-indexed attribute layers and shapefiles for geospatial data sets.


/


Intuitively, one might reason that code included in data sets should in general 
be implemented in a style (and programming languages) commonplace in the 
research community which would primarily assess and reuse a research object.  
Among the natural sciences, for example, the most widely used languages 
include C++, Python, R, Java, and Matlab.  

However, there are also nontrivial reasons why distinct programming 
environments should be developed specifically for the "/research object/" 
context (again, taking this term as descriptive and not limited to 
the explicit Research Object specification).  For one thing, 
there are requirements (or at least desirable characteristics) of 
data-publication context code that is different from other 
scientific and software environments.  For example, FAIRsharing 
principles suggest that authors should be particular attuned to 
the accessibility and interoperability of published data.  Scientists 
may have special-purpose software and equipment in their labs 
(or workplace) but many readers interested in a thorough 
study of their work may not have the same technology on hand, 
especially if it is based on closed-source and/or commercial software.
Authors may indeed take steps to help scientists who 
*/do/* have access to the best software properly 
access and reust data publications (e.g., by documenting 
all steps necessarily to load data files into scientific 
applications similar to those the authors themselves employed), 
but data sets should in general be prepared under the assumption 
that some readers will need to take advantage of published 
materials through freely-available, open-source components 
(which may entail partial or scaled-back reuse or functionality).  

Moreover, the basic capabilities of loading/deserializing 
raw files often depends on special libraries maintained 
by relatively small groups (as mentioned above).  Data-encoding 
formats tend to evolve as the relevant science progresses, 
so deserialization code often needs relatively frequent 
updates (not to mention that language versions and third-party 
libraries can become outdated).  Deserialization code therefore 
must be kept in sync both with specifications of the relevant 
encoding format and with dependencies on the overall toolchain 
(compilers, system kernels, etc.).  Duplicating such 
efforts among a multiplicity of operating systems and 
languages makes maintenance a more expansive task.  A plausible 
solution is the dissemination of a single deserialization 
component (e.g., a Virtual Machine) which is sufficiently 
autonomous to run in a multitude of host environments, 
such that deserialization libaries can be targeted for 
that specific component at least as a precursor to other 
implementations (maintainers might still prepare 
native-compiled libraries, for example, but a 
canonical VM-based version could provide a stopgap 
while such components are under development).

A further consideration is that dataset-related code 
spans a variety of distinct use-cases, as listed 
earlier.  A potential outcome is tension between 
competing priorities of implementing all 
dataset code in a single programming language 
and, on the other hand, writing code in each 
area of functionality in a style most conducive 
to its own requirements.

This paper assumes that a plausible solution to these questions 
involves a dedicated Virtual Machine and Compiler Infrastructure 
that would be explicit designed around data publication, 
research objects (as a general concept), and translational 
science (or, more broadly, interdisciplinary approach which 
brodge, or have the potential to migrate between, 
pure and applied research, as well as natural science and 
humanistic/social science foci and implementations).

A further presupposition is that such compiler and VM 
components can and should be implemented in a mostly 
self-contained manner, with as few external dependencies 
as possible.  In particular, the entire code base 
for these tools should be isolated and, potentially, 
distributed alongside any code specific to a 
single data set.  Anyone with access to the 
overall package would therefore have the necessary 
software environment available to run included code.  
Of course, the compiler/VM components would need to be 
built from source, so users would need, for instance, 
a typical C++ toolchain.  But such affordances 
might be necessary to run other dataset code anyhow.

Providing an integrated environment for running 
dataset code does not imply that all such code 
must be written in a single language.  Full-text 
searches might be expressed in code that reads 
more like a query language, in contrast to a 
more script-style deserialization library.  
Plugins to host applications loading data sets 
might be written in a declarative style 
emphasizing cross-component interop, alluding 
to (e.g.) the Common Workflow Language or various 
protocol-definition formats.  It is a reasonable 
goal, however, to design a VM that would be a 
common */compilation target/* even if surface-level 
code is expressed in terms consistent with the 
relevant area of functionality (query versus script versus, 
say, workflow definition).  Of course, if not constrained 
by existing language standards, a single front-end 
language could adopt a flexible syntax that supports 
multiple functionality-related styles 
(consider LINQ as a DSL vis-a-vis C#).

In any case, this paper will assume that creating such a 
compiler and VM toolchain is a worthwhile project, and 
will discuss certain featuers and implementation details that 
reflect the specific goals of those hypothetical 
components.  In particular, the compiler/VM would be 
specifically built to support Research Object development 
and facilitate data-sharing in multi-disciplinary and 
translational contexts.  I will cover several 
topics, including VM design and instruction sets, 
front-end parsing, intermediate representations, 
static analysis, interop with native-compiled procedures, 
embedding the toolchain in host applications, 
and taking steps to ensure that the toolchain 
can be built with minimal external dependencies.

For sake of exposition, the discussion will start with 
perspectives particularly relevent to full-text search 
and text encoding.  These themes may seem tangential 
to typical topics in compiler theory, but they 
serve as a useful precursor to further discussion of 
the more-familiar topics.  Subsequent sections 
will address such issues as object lifetimes, 
calling conventions, resource (de-)allocation, design-by-contract, 
dependent types, and other topics that are 
currently at the forefront of programming-language theory.


Full-Text Search and Representation

The term "/full-text/" search is defined in contrast to 
document portals where users may only access abstracts and 
excerpts from published manuscripts.  By implication, 
in full-text search the entire content of a book are 
article is available to match against query terms.  
This does not automatically mean that all the text 
is accessible to all users; parts of documents might 
be behind a paywall even if full-text is included 
in the search process.  Also, queries might 
be evaluated against compressed files (or 
database records) that are distinct from 
document content as undertood when reading 
from beginning to end.  A "/vector/" datanase, 
for instance, might only record the number 
of times a given word (or phrase) occurs in a 
given resource, on the assumption that 
more mentions imply greater relevance.  This is, 
of course, a crudy metric; more granular 
models of relevance might distinguish, for 
example, a publication arguing */for/* some 
theory, versus */against/*, or neutrally 
*/evaluation/* pro and con.  

For sake of discussion, it is reasonable to assume 
that full-text search capabilities */do/* include 
matching input strings against document text 
in its entirety.  Ideally, a bibliographic database 
will support relevance-based queries as well, and 
--- in the best case scenario --- author annotations, 
or some other sentiment/rhetorical declaration method, 
will offer a more granular model of inter-textual 
relationships (how one document comments on, 
endorses, rebufs, etc., some other text, or theory, 
or line of argument).  If nothing else, relevance 
matches could provide an initial pool of candidates 
that are further analyzed.  But in many cases 
exact phrase-match is indeed the best strategy for 
query evaluation, notwithstanding how the semantics 
of a passage can overlap with those of a search phrase 
even without the query content appearing as such 
%-- the words might occur partially, or in a different 
order, or replaced by (near-)synonyms.  Such possibilities 
imply that brute-force matching is often less than ideal, 
but still, sometimes a reader is trying to re-locate a 
sentence or paragraph they had seen sometime before, and 
remember specific words therein.  Or, users are reasonably 
confident that any relevant passage will have an exact 
match with just one or two query variants (e.g., first-name-last-name 
for a person or vice-versa).  In still other cases, technical 
terms and idioms are often embraced precisely because they 
yield a canonical vocabulary that one can expect to 
find in relevant publications.  A manuscript which has 
nontrivial discussion about the Rust "/borrow checker/", for 
instance, will almost certainly use that exact phrase somewhere.  

With that said, it can be surpringly complicated to establish 
what exactly */is/* a manuscript text.  The most common 
format for a person just */reading/* publications is 
PDF, but PDF viewers' internal text representation 
differens from what users "/think/" they're reading 
in some important ways.  One obvious artifact of 
the digital (or printed) page is hyphenation %--  
but PDF also introduces anomalies via ligatures; 
inconsistent spacing; mixing header/footer, sidebar, 
and other "/marginal/" content with the primary 
text; and potentially error-prone treatment 
of characters with special typesetting or presentation 
requirements.  PDF software can compensate for such 
issues to varying degrees, but in general searches against 
documents in PDF format should not be performed via 
viewers' internal search functionality, or against 
document text as reported by PDF components 
(e.g., poppler's get-page-text ...).  

PDFs, of course, are generated from files of some other type.  
This might be LaTeX; or, more recently, XML-based 
formats such as JATS or BITS.  Searching against 
well-structured (e.g., XML-based) representations 
presumably offers more consistent results than targeting 
text as seen by PDF applications internally.  
Unfortunately, current publishing practice does 
not include disseminating JATS (for instance) as a 
supplemental resource sibling to PDF files, for full-text 
query purposes.  There seems to be no technical 
arguments */against/* sharing XML (or similar) 
manuscript encodings, however.  Of course, 
not all users of search capabilities will have 
documents on hand to begin with; they may perform a search 
seeking to find relevant content ab initio.  Obviously, 
though, document archives can store at least 
moderately-sized collections of machine-readable 
encodings and execute the same search algorithms 
therewith as a sophisticated PDF viewer might perform 
were PDF files augmented with associated (or 
embedded) machine-readable text encoding.fn: with 
the caveat that queries which take a comparatively 
long time to evaluate on a single manuscript are 
obviously impractical against thousands or 
millions of candidates.  This is why vector-database 
relevance methods may be important as an initial 
step; or why publishers are advised to maintain 
smaller-scale collections of materials that share a 
similar topic, or subdivide their collections 
in such a manner, so that user can 
explicitly constrain their searches to 
collections with a specific semantic marker.

Even granted that full-text representations are available, 
however, leaves unresolved numerous issues with respect to 
what content is actually modeled for text-search 
purposes.  Authors and typically readers will usually 
have a pretty clear picture about the basic components 
of sentences and individual words, distinguishing 
such information from artifacts of layout and presentation.  
For instance, hyphenation is not an intrinsic 
part of text content, and two parts of a hyphenated word 
should be fused together when building machine-readable 
encodings.  Likewise, character-sequences such as 
"/ffi/" might have the visual appearance of a ligature 
character (Unicode ?? in this case) but the underlying 
content is merely those three Ascii/Latin1 letters.  

There are other, subtler cases, however.  For example, 
footnote markers, citations, number or bullet icons 
for enumerations, and emendments within quoted 
material (ellipses, square brackets, and so forth) may or 
may not be content that a user deems "/part/" of a sentence.  
This could manifest in multiple ways.  In the case of an 
exact-match query with a given search string, presumably 
details such as the presence of a footnote marker 
after an intermediate word would not affect whether a 
portion of a sentence properly satisfies the match 
(relevant to the user's intentions; although there 
may be exceptions here, such as when the query 
string originates from a copy-paste in a PDF viewer).
On the other hand, if the user wants to */copy/* 
a specific sentence they may wish to include 
foonote markers, ellipses inside quoted material, 
etc.  At least, copy actions might be performed 
through a dialog box or other GUI component 
that allows specifying options (whether of not 
to include footnotes, etc.).  

Further considerations involve how basic discursive 
units such as sentences are to be modeled.  
Most text has straightforward subdivision into 
sections, paragraphs, sentences, etc.  But sometimes 
the hierarchy is more ambiguous; a footnote whose 
marker lies in the interior of a sentence might 
itself span multiple sentences, which thereby 
exhibit a kind of parent/child relationship 
with the one in the main text.  Similarly, 
sentences within block (or sometimes inline) quotes 
or enumerations function in some regards as children 
of the sentence through which that material is 
introduced.  If the latter sentence (or part thereof) 
ends with a colon rather than a period, it may be a 
judgment call as to whether the beginning of 
the relevant quote/enumeration is the start of a 
new sentence or a continuation of the previous one.

Another set of questions relates to styling and 
presentation features such as italics, hyperrefs, 
figure captions, etc.  A robust 
search functionality might allow users to explicitly 
request matches in italicized text (let's say they 
remember a passage, include its being 
in slanted script, but not which page contained it).
Nonetheless, in a typical case users presumably 
will not distinguish font-face when deeming a 
phrase-match to be accurate.  The case might 
be different vis-a-vis copying the text of a 
sentence, constructing PDF comment boxes, and 
so forth.  A reasonable question is how users would 
want result text presented, if, for example, a 
query asks for the contents of one specific 
sentence, or if the "/query/" is actually a 
response to gestures, such as selecting a 
copy-sentence option from a context menu.  
Plausible encoding formats specifically 
for query-results (in this sense) could be 
HTML, Markdown (or similar), plain text 
(ignoring details such as italics), etc. 
(of course, specifying which of these 
is desired could be part of the query parameters).  
Even if presentation facets such as italics vs. 
normalfont are ignored, query results would still need 
to consider how to represent quotations %-- use the 
same quote marks as the document, or revert to 
simplier Latin1 quotes (which do not distinguish 
left and wrote), or employ some encoding to 
surround quoted materials with tags (or analogous 
markers); and, should block quotes (since 
they are indicated by marginal indentation rather 
than quote characters) be handled  
differently from inline?

There may not be definitive rules to resolve all 
such issues.  The larger point is that text-encoding 
is not a trivial matter of preserving intermediate 
files, but rather that authors (or editors) should 
consciously mold machine-readable representations 
with deliberate acknowledgment of where principles 
may be ambiguous (such as what qualifies as a 
sentence-boundary) or development of encoding 
protocols that give end-users choice when there are 
nultiple reasonable options.  Of course, publishers and 
digital editors can develop default "/house/" styles 
to which authors might defer %-- pondering these 
minutiae should not have to be a precondition for 
submitting manuscripts %-- but in any case a 
self-conscious protocol or meta-model for text 
encoding could still be part of the document-preparation pipeline.

Given this background, we should assume that any "/machine-readable 
text encoding/" will include a set of rules for 
how variegated phenomenon (not just words and characters) 
are to be modeled (e.g., sentence boundaries and 
inter-sentence relationships and hierarchies) and detailed 
consideration of potential ambiguities or underdetermination 
within search and text-access (such as copy/paste) functionality  
(meaning that users, perhaps via GUI components, 
or query-parameters fill in details to resolve questions 
where results might be presented in multiple ways).  A thorough 
text-encoding standard would thereby be relatively complex, 
perhaps more so than suggested by current 
technologies.  For example, the Journal Article 
Tag Suite (JATS) has a fairly comprehensive 
vocabulary of XML elements, but most of these 
are designed for preparatory information 
declared before the start of actual text 
(JAPS supports a wide range of metadata 
related to bibliographic info, provenance, 
copyright, and so forth).  

It's worth noting in this context that most text-encoding 
standards are built around */paragraphs/* as the basic 
units of discourse.  The BioC format (used by a 
subset of PubMed Central), and Text Encoding Initiative 
(TEI), have !<s> or !<sentence> tags available, but 
even here these are not guaranteed to be present in documents.
LaTex has rules for determining sentence boundaries 
in normal cases (and macros to override those defaults 
when necessary), but these most simplistic conventions 
do not address all (or even most) sentence-related 
ambiguities, and moreover they have no impact 
on document preparation except for subtle 
spacing policies (specifically, unless so-called "/french/" 
spacing is indicated, PDF and other generated publications 
will have a small extra space between the punctuation 
ending one sentence and the start of the next, as 
compared to the normal inter-word space on the 
relevant line of print).  Without extra packages, 
one cannot use the LaTeX pipeline to save sentence-level 
metadata (e.g., the PDF page coordinates of sentences's 
start and end points, which in theory can be tracked 
via the pgfpos commands and auxiliary files).

In short, in almost all publishing workflows there 
is little effort made to introduces individual 
sentences as a recognized discursive unit.  
Similar points can be made about repositories 
such as CORD19, a collection of open-access 
documents about Covid and Coronaviruses, first curated 
by the Allen Institute for AI (AI2) in the early months 
of the pandemic.  CORD19 was engineered with accmmodation 
for limitations in publising practice %-- in particular, 
most articles were accessed only in PDF form, so CORD19 
had to rely on error-prone transcription of internal 
PDF representations to create (ostensibly) machine-readable 
text encoding.  AI2 explicitly recognized this 
limitation and even issued a "/call to arms/" encouraging 
publishers to create more rigorous standards.  But, in any 
case, the JSON format adopted by CORD19 broke down 
text content only to the level of individual paragraphs.  
Finer-grained details (e.g., in the context of annotations) 
were modeled via "/standoff/" markup (using character-indices 
to declare start and end point, where each paragraph is understood 
as an internally-indexable stream); however, that system was 
not extended to sentence-boundaries themselves.

It is not entirely clear why so few formats are built around 
the sentence (rather than paragraph) scale, especially because 
many queries or search scenarios would seem to implicitly 
rely on sentence identification.  For example, it would be 
quite reasonable for a query to request matches wherein 
two distinct terms are both present in the same sentence.  
Also, in many cases a credible default response 
convention for queries would be to return the full 
text of a sentence where matches occur (this is just as 
plausible as returning a "/window/" around the match 
hits dilated by several words on either side).  Tracking individual 
sentences could also be valuable in use-cases such as 
PDF comment boxes and "/track change/" functionality.  Visibly comparing 
two versions of a manuscript, say, could be restricted to showing 
sentences which differ between the two (or also, maybe, 
sentences present in one but not the other).  Similarly, 
suppose someone using a PDF file creates a comment box 
to indicate an edit that they recommend.  With proper 
metadata about page coordinates, the PDF viewer could 
obtain an identifier for the specific sentence 
wherein the comment box is pinned, and such 
id could even be included in the comment text itself 
(maybe in the style of a social-media handle) so that 
"/track changes/" software could cross-reference the 
PDF without intermediate sources (e.g., XML files).

Even if sentence-level encoding is not currently 
the norm, there is no technical reason why it 
would not become more prevalent in the future.  
Properly identifying almost all boundaries 
can literally be a simple as employing two 
spaces instead of one in input sources, with 
special notation needed only in rare anomalies 
(another plausible solution is to adopt LaTeX 
conventions that distinguish periods as punctuation 
versus in abbreviations in most normal cases, 
deferring to \SlashAt{} macros only on occasion; 
although here the need for special notation 
arises more frequently than with a two-space-versus-one 
rule).  In any case, the remainder of this discussion 
will assume that text encoding standards are 
adopted (or developed) which treat sentences as 
the basic units, and paragraphs as compound 
objects (for lack of a canonical term, I will 
call this "/sentence-level encoding/").

Much of the preceding material serves 
mostly as a preamble to this section's primary 
theme, which is the best structure to employ 
for natural-language markup.  Numerous 
(at least partially) theoretical discussions exist 
about the unique challenges for defining machine-readable 
text representations, and which generic formats 
(XML, JSON, something else %-- SGML, even) are best suited 
for this case.  Although LaTeX has long been used for 
typesetting specifically, publishers currently appear to 
prefer XML-based formats (particularly JATS and its 
derivates, including BITS %-- the Book Interchange Tag Suite) 
as the primary intermediary files (grounding the 
central processes of a workflow flanked, on one side, 
by documents actually submitted by authors %-- in formats 
such as Microsoft Word %-- and on the other by publications 
read by humans, in PDF or, for instance, HTML).  

At the same time, bear in mind that XML is itself essentially a 
simplified version of SGML which was in use for many years in 
academic/technical publishing, and some programmers argue that 
features excluded from XML %-- notably, concurrant 
markup %-- are still useful for text-encoding purposes.  There 
are, then, contrarian models that are self-consciously 
incompatible with XML and present a more complex markup 
conception which, in principle, may be more effective for 
describing the contents of text documents.  XML, of course, 
is not explicitly designed for representing long text 
*/documents/*, like books or articles (nor are other generic 
formats, such as JSON).  There are subtleties in the 
nature of human language, in its written form, that 
require special treatment irregardless of how the 
information is encoded overall.  To put it differently, 
natural-language text is not just a "/data structure/"; or, 
if we do see it as such (on the premise that almost 
"/anything/" is a data structure) it is one with 
intricate details and corner-cases.

Among the XML alternatives that have been proposed 
(and implemented, to varying degrees) a noteworthy 
case if TagML (the Text-as-Graph markup language) 
which features concurrent markup, among other 
generalizations (from the simple document hierarchy 
evinced in XML).  Concurrency means that multiple 
hierarchical structures can exist in parallel; 
the start of one element (e.g., a start tag) may 
exist after the start but before the end of some 
other element, while the former's end outlasts the 
latter's.  That is, in some instances elements 
exhibit overlapping rather than parent-child, sibling, 
or disjoint relationships.  For a concrete example, 
when recording poetry it may be desirable to adopt, 
as basic units, both sentences and verse-lines.  
One line may include the second half of one sentence 
and first half of the next.  Concurrent markup 
may therefore be an intrinsic feature of certain 
academic domains, although the relevant 
structures could be enulated in "/pure/" XML 
via "/landmarks/" (zero-width elements that, in pairs, mark 
beginning and end milestones for some state or condition 
that cannot be represented by XML tags directly).  
Meanwhile, in exceptional circumstances for ordinary 
manuscripts (not just verse) there can be similar requirements 
to identify printed lines as a distinct markup 
layer.fn: { One example involves fine-tuning PDFs created 
by, for instance, LaTeX; suppose we have a worflow 
where XML (or similar) encoding applies for the 
main text but LaTeX is still used for typesetting 
the final PDFs.  The LaTeX-to-PDF generators 
(anecdotally, at least) can be problemmatic 
when it comes to handling (i.e., creating) 
widows or orphans, both in the case of words and of 
lines (by "/anecdotally/" I mean this is something 
I have found repeatedly to be a pain in the arse).  
In some cases authors/editors can get better results 
by manually manipulating the LaTeX rubber-length 
mechanism (which usually works well) and that 
sometimes involves stipulating that some precise 
string of words or characters should be exactly 
one double-justified print line, which of course 
might overlap with but neither include nor stand inside 
one sentence.  In this case line/sentence 
concurrency becomes a factor even in mundane prose. }  
Apart from concurrency, TagML also differs from 
XML in that attribute values (attributes are 
string-indexed, so the "/keys/" are structurally 
isomoporphic to XML) are not (necessarily) 
simple strings, but can potentially have the same spectrum 
of (semi)-hierarchical content as the overall 
document (according to the TagML standard, each 
attribute value is itself a root note).  

Debating the relative merits of JATS, for example, versus 
a non-XML alternative (such as TagML) %-- and whether 
these considerations justifying departing from 
XML entirely as opposed to emulating other structures 
via conventions such as landmark/milestone tags %-- 
is outside the scope of this paper.  I */will/* 
comment that XML has become entrenched not only 
due to the format itself but also associated 
languages and tooling, such as XPath, XQuery, 
XSLT, XQSE (XQuery Scripting Extensions) and the 
XML Document Object Model.  Projects that 
envision an XML alternative need to address the 
possibilities of similar */associated/* languages 
as well.  In the case of TagML, for example, 
there does not appear to be a concomitant 
format for querying TagML files analogous to 
XQuery, or a way of individuation parts of 
documents in the sense of XPath.  Articulating a 
non-XML paradigm, however well-motivated, 
is only a first step; on that basis one 
would then need to progress to replacements 
for DOM, XQuery, etc.

But the main point I intent to emphasize %-- which 
the XML/TagML contrast exemplifies nicely %-- is that 
the superficial structures of any format are not 
the primary factor when considering which languages are best 
suited for text representation.  The important 
difference from markup with or without concurrent 
tag-overlap is not whether start/end tag pairs are 
consistently nested in source files.  The more significant 
implications come to the fore when one is writing code 
to traverse, navigate, transform, and isolate/extrast 
specific parts or values from the represented 
data structures.  In the case of XML, for example, 
one maneuvers around documents by visiting children 
(or parent) nodes by turn, and/or sibling nodes 
in sequence.  Simple hierarchy makes such navigation 
straightforward.  With concurrent markup such as 
TagML, code that traverses a document 
looking for some specific content (or apply transforms 
to most/all elements) has to address a wider variety of 
cases.  When elements start inside others but end outside, 
determining the proper sequence for visiting nodes is 
(at least potentially) more complex.  This is not an argument 
against, say, TagML %-- conceivably such complexity 
actually makes traversal easier to implement, because it 
brings complex cases to the forefront rather than 
addressing them obliquely, such as when overlap is 
emulated indirectly in XML via landmarks, since in the second 
cases programmers might be more likely to code up ad-hoc 
tools to keep track of data relationships basically 
sutured on to the XML foundation (e.g., maintaining a 
separate stack-object to keep track of start/end milestones).  
But it does mean that the best format, all else 
being equal, is the one which is most conducive 
to intuitive implementation of traversal/navigation 
code.  

In short, the distinguishing factor of any markup format 
involves how code might move from one node or "/site/" 
in a document to another, as well as how it extracts 
information on the current "/focus/" site.  For instance, 
in XML, the main "/sites/" are element nodes and the principle 
operations available on any given such node are reading 
the tagname, attribute values, and the element's text 
content (if any).  The primary */navigation/* possibilities 
are moving from parent to (first) child node, and from 
(any) child to parent, as well as between sibling nodes.  
The crucial details of XML's specific format is that such 
traversal possibilities are presumed from the 
outset, and navigation/extraction code can orient 
itself against this architectural paradigm.

For sake of discussion, commpare these XML conventions 
with a graph database; let's say, a property-hypergraph 
model which supports at least two levels of (hyper-)nodes 
(hypernodes are, in general, a tuple of nodes at the 
lower level) as well as labeled edges between hypernodes, and 
key/value attribute lists available for multiple kinds 
(nodes, hypernodes, edges, etc.).  Edges themselves 
may actually be "/triples/" where a directed edge (in addition 
to its source and target hypernode) has its hypernode 
(the middle slot in the subject-predicate-object triple model), 
although the nodes specific to hyperedges may carry the 
stipulation of a "/label/" attribute that conforms to 
some Ontology.  Also, it is reasonable to assume that 
any hypernode can be referenced via a globally unique 
identifier, which in turn may be the value of a node, 
so nodes can act as proxies or handles to hypernodes, 
permitting containment hierarchies */de facto/* 
(even if hypernodes can only nest one level of contained 
nodes, those nodes may proxy other hypernodes, and so 
on iteratively).

This schematic picture of property-hypergraph database theory 
(which does not corespond to actual engines in use, necessarily) 
is tangential to my discussion, but it illustrates a noticeably 
*/different/* conception of navigation and traversal compared 
to XML.  Visitor code could switch focus from a hypernode 
to a node contained therein; or from one hypernode to another 
via a labeled edge (maybe following one specific label 
as defined by an Ontology or controlled vocabulary); or, 
alternatively, migrate to a different hypernode via a 
node-proxy (equivalent to moving between XML elements by 
reading the destination's id from an attribute value).  
Code could also read a property-value at the current 
site, or read "/predicate/" details (in the subject/predicate/object 
triple sense) from an edge-annotation.  While some of these 
protocols vaguely resemble XML, the details are different.  
XML has no equivalent to edge-triples, for example.  On the other 
hand, each XML element potentially has its own text content, 
whereas in the graph model sketched here text would only belong 
to a node as an integral whole (without internal/nested elements).

There are a wide variety of domain-specific formats, like 
DICOM (for medical images) and IFC (Industry Foundation Classes, 
for engineering) that have their own variation on 
themes familiar to, say, XML.  For example, IFC distinguishes 
*/attributes/* from */properties/*, where the latter are more 
free-form.  Document-type schemas can restrict (and require) 
the presence and scope of attributes, but sources can 
introduce idiosyncratic properties with few restrictions.  
A similar distinction is achieved in DICOM via "/public/" 
versus "/private/" element-labels (DICOM has the further 
detail that field labels, which function in effect like 
attribute keys, in DICOM have both string 
and number codes; private attributes employ odd numbers).  
DICOM also uses a two-part-code system (somewhat comparable to 
XML namespaces).  Each of these formats evince algorithmic 
differences in terms of traversing and extracting information 
from documents.

Structural differences of these sorts are sufficiently 
widespread that it is helpful to refer to them 
in general, and existing jargon (such as traversal 
or navigation, or terms related to the "/visitor pattern/") 
do not cover proper generality.  For sake of 
exposition, here I borrow the term "/topomorphic/" (which 
apparently exists in chemistry but not, usually, computer 
science).  Formats such as XML, TagML, DICOM, IFC, differ 
in terms of what I propose to call */topomorphic regimes/", 
in that there are subtle (or not-so-subtle) differences 
in the computational processes involved in moving from 
site to site and extracting data thereby.  A central idea 
here is */site/* itself, which I envision to be a 
generic concept for a "/place/" in a structured document 
%-- one that could be the current value of a source-code 
variable or object %-- such that code can, via 
one "/foreground/" or "/focal/" site, read specific 
kinds of information (an attribute value, tag name, 
element text, etc.).  Moreover, there are specific 
options to "/move/" from one site to another 
(e.g., by reassigning the value of a source-code 
object to point to a different node).  The data 
available at a given site, plus other sites immediately 
accessible therefrom, could be called the "/neighborhood/" 
of a site.  Where formats differ is the parameters of the 
"/shape/" of such neighborhoods.  For instance, the neighborhood 
of an XML node includes its parent and child nodes, previous and next 
siblings, and its attributes and text content.  Given the 
differences outlined above between XML and TagML, the neighborhood 
of a TagML node would be structurally different.  In terminology 
I adopt here, these are */topomorphic/* differences and 
illustrate how XML and TagML exhibit/implement distinct "/topomorphic regimes/".


    


 

    
 
     

  
  


 
   


  
   






   
 

 
       
  

  


   




  










